ular so it's  an oil painting i really liked it when i  first saw it because i feel as though  the movement is just perfect as this  moment before the wave crashes the  curvature of it the feeling that it  gives me almost as though i can hear the  wave as it's crashing down and also the  fact that the colors are really rich and  deep i adore i don't know if it's day or  night light or dark sad or happy so i  can interpret it either way every time  that i'm looking at it depending on how  i'm feeling so it really really  transports me somewhere very powerful  and that's why it's also called for your  eyes only it's meant to represent this  peaceful solitude  we all have the ability to make people  feel the way that we felt when we were  younger is such a powerful quote from dr  kirk conda because i think it really  shows that the impact of our childhood  and how that can affect us and i think  that when we're around people people  have a vibe they have a very easy  ability to make you feel a certain way  and that can oscillate wildly from  feeling almost abusive and negative to  something just feeling like tender  warmth and peace and i think that no  matter what our childhoods we're like  once we are adults i think that we have  a very strong responsibility to undo any  sort of trauma or damage that we were  given when we were younger and to make  the world a better place for ourselves  and for other people so the way that  this ties with this painting is that  this is a painting of venus which was  the goddess of beauty and love in  ancient greece and ancient rome and she  had an interesting birth story because  she was born in fully adult form so in  this painting as we see her she looks  like an adult but technically she's just  a baby because she's just arrived so  what this means to me is kind of the  view that even though i can see her as  being an adult  technically deep down she still has  those same needs for love and affection  and attention and these desi